    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "I never studied math beyond primary school, so my relationship with it is mostly one of curiosity and simple interest. I enjoy the logic in physics and chemistry, which are more practical. I don’t feel confident in advanced math, but I’m open to learning. I see math as a tool, not a subject I need to master deeply.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Yes, sometimes. When I hear about complex equations or abstract concepts, I feel a bit nervous. It’s not about being bad at math, but I don’t have a strong foundation. My anxiety comes from not knowing how to approach problems, especially when they’re unfamiliar. I try to calm myself by breaking things down into simple steps.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "Yes, I used a simple AI app to help me understand basic algebra. I typed in problems and got step-by-step explanations. It was helpful for visualizing how to solve equations. I found it easy to use and reassuring. It didn’t replace my learning, but it made me feel less lost when starting over.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "First, write the equation in standard form: ax² + bx + c = 0. Then, use the quadratic formula: x = (-b ± √(b² - 4ac)) / (2a). Calculate the discriminant to see if there are real solutions. If it’s positive, two real roots; if zero, one; if negative, no real roots. This is the main method I know, even if I didn’t learn it deeply.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "To find stationary points, take the derivative of y with respect to x, f'(x). Set f'(x) = 0 and solve for x. These x-values are candidates for stationary points. Plug them back into the original function to get y-values. Then, check if they are maxima, minima, or points of inflection using the second derivative test.",
